---
// Project page for governed agentic empirical research. Current writing is
// private; this page carries only the public research digest and project context.
import Base from "../../../layouts/Base.astro";
import lifecycle from "../../../data/governed-agentic-research/ai-in-empirical-se/sources.json";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const research = `${base}research/`;
const soa = `${base}research/soa-agentic-se/`;
const track = `${base}research/governed-agentic-research/ai-in-empirical-se/`;
---

<Base
  annotatable
  title="Jason Cusati — Governed Agentic Empirical Research"
  description="Research on governing AI-assisted empirical software engineering: provenance, bounded agent authority, typed claims and reproducible checks."
>
  <p class="crumbs"><a href={research}>← Research</a></p>
  <h1>Governed Agentic Empirical Research</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Governing AI-assisted empirical software engineering research</p>
  <p class="lede">
    Research on what must be recorded and checked when AI agents participate in
    empirical software engineering research: which decisions are delegated, what
    evidence supports them, and how the process stays reviewable.
  </p>

  <h2>The research digest</h2>
  <p>
    The public digest maps what has been published on LLMs and agents at each
    stage of empirical software engineering research — question design,
    literature reviews, repository mining, qualitative and quantitative
    analysis, artifact generation and reporting — and what those sources say
    about rigor, validity, reproducibility and the human role. It is anchored on
    Gomes et al. (2026) and backed by a source explorer and dated method note.
  </p>
  <div class="cards">
    <a class="card" href={track}>
      <span class="card-title">AI and agents across the empirical-SE lifecycle</span>
      <span class="card-desc">The synthesis, the {lifecycle.length}-record source explorer, the Consensus searches and the literature-gap table.</span>
      <span class="card-meta">{lifecycle.length} sources · 6 October 2026</span>
    </a>
  </div>

  <h2>How this relates to the other projects</h2>
  <p>
    This project is the methodological layer on top of work that is reported
    elsewhere on this site. Each project below supplies one strand of the
    argument; none of them is the whole research programme.
  </p>
  <ul class="relations">
    <li>
      <a href={`${soa}agentic-harnesses/`}>Agentic harnesses</a> and
      <a href={`${soa}agentic-memory/`}>Agent memory</a> (the
      <a href={soa}>State of the Art in Agentic SE</a> project) are themselves
      governed research passes: schema-constrained source records, discovery
      separated from an adversarially prompted verification pass, provenance
      fields on every record. They are the method's literature-review stage in
      practice — one stage of the lifecycle, not the whole process.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Research.AI</strong> (the <code>agentic-kg</code> project under
      the knowledge-graphs tenet) supplies the sharpest case for human
      methodological judgement: reconciling a gold-label schema where the
      labelling choice itself could distort measured precision and recall, so
      the evaluation would measure the researchers' decision rather than the
      system's behaviour.
    </li>
    <li>
      A time-boxed empirical study run in August 2026 under the framework's
      tooling (<code>agentic-governance</code>, <code>agentic-research</code>)
      is one of this project's two worked cases: typed claims, pre-registered
      thresholds, a human re-derivation ledger and an adversarial reviewer
      gating what could be reported. Changes that came out of it flowed back
      into both tools.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Status</h2>
  <p>
    The research digest on AI across the empirical-SE lifecycle is public (219
    sources, 6 October 2026). Current writing is private.
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  .crumbs { font-size: 0.9rem; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .lede { font-size: 1.1rem; color: var(--color-muted); margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .subtitle { font-family: var(--font-body); color: var(--color-muted); margin: -0.5rem 0 1rem; }
  .cards { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr)); gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0; }
  .card { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 0.4rem; padding: 1rem 1.1rem; border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-left: 4px solid var(--color-accent); border-radius: 4px; background: var(--color-surface); color: var(--color-text); text-decoration: none; }
  .card:hover { border-left-color: var(--color-link); text-decoration: none; }
  .card-title { font-family: var(--font-body); font-weight: 600; color: var(--color-link); font-size: 1.05rem; }
  .card-desc { font-size: 0.92rem; color: var(--color-muted); }
  .card-meta { font-family: var(--font-body); font-size: 0.78rem; color: var(--color-muted); margin-top: 0.25rem; }
  .relations li { margin-bottom: 0.75rem; }
  code { font-family: var(--font-mono); font-size: 0.9em; background: var(--color-surface); padding: 0 0.25em; }
</style>
